# Instructions

Locate the matching notes/commentary heading for checks.{{ name }}. Does it begin a separate section in the MAIN READING SEQUENCE, rather than an ancillary footnote area underneath continuing main prose?

Use the full local_raw_evidence and column geometry. Follow ordinary column reading order: the left column can finish a discussion and the right column can then start its notes section at the top. Main prose and notes sharing a physical page does not make the notes a page-bottom apparatus.

A notes section may start near the end of one column and continue in the next. Its entries can explain and cite the preceding discussion: that relationship is normal for endnotes and does not itself make them ancillary footnotes.

In contrast, a small note area below ongoing main prose, separated from that prose in its own footer stream, is ancillary even if headed Notes. A chapter title, numbering, small type, the word Notes alone, or an assumed unseen separator cannot establish a new main section. Determine the actual location in the column reading sequence.

This asks about the SECTION START only, not the spelling, paragraph joins or correctness of individual notes. If no matching heading and independent section sequence can be established, answer FALSE.

# True

A matching heading starts a distinct notes/commentary section in the main reading sequence.

# False

That independent main-sequence section start is not established; it may instead be an ancillary note area.
